% conclusion.tex — compressed for 8-page limit

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Topic shortcut and label equivalence.}
Three observations argue against a topic shortcut: unstable topic OOD (F1\,=\,0.541$\pm$0.207), scrambled labels matching correct within 3.9\,pp, and label-free MLM detection (F1\,$\geq$\,0.97 at $K \leq 5$). The scrambled-correct equivalence could reflect classification regularizing representations independently of label content, or random permutation over 9 classes preserving enough correlations; distinguishing these requires varying class count. Topic classification occasionally approaches MLM on ActorAttack (0.625 vs.\ 0.893) despite misaligned labels, reflecting domain exposure rather than the topic objective.

\paragraph{Single-LLM confound.}
ToxicChat (+18--42\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:human_data}) and cross-LLM experiments (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}) confirm transfer. Mixed-LLM 9-class maintains DD F1\,=\,0.818$\pm$0.096 (+53\,pp) while MLM collapses; one hypothesis is that MLM captures LLM-specific surface patterns while classification extracts style-invariant features. Cross-LLM error decomposition confirms F1 degradation is driven by false positives on unfamiliar benign text, not missed attacks.

\paragraph{Variance reversal on human data.}
Classification supervision reduces variance 4--20$\times$ over MLM on synthetic OOD (3 seeds); on ToxicChat this reverses (std\,=\,0.087 vs.\ 0.013).
Frozen probing localizes MLM instability to Stage-2: frozen MLM outperforms the full pipeline on ActorAttack (0.941 vs.\ 0.893), while 9-class frozen matches (0.972; Appendix~\ref{app:frozen_verify}).

\paragraph{Encoder capacity, temporal, and lexical analysis.}
Three encoders (125M--355M) differ in pretraining; same-architecture scaling is needed. Mean pooling matches BiGRU and shuffling produces $|\Delta| < 1$\,pp (Section~\ref{sec:decomposition}), confirming order-invariant detection. TF-IDF collapse on ActorAttack (0.261 vs.\ 0.961 on DD) demonstrates beyond-lexical features (Section~\ref{sec:limitations}).

\section{Conclusion}

Classification-based DAPT is the most robust adaptation for base-size encoders; unsupervised pretraining closes $\sim$60\,pp but collapses under mixed-LLM training. Within the architectures tested, larger encoders need less adaptation; 4--20$\times$ variance reduction reverses on human data. Three negative results---label correctness, temporal order, domain specificity---constrain which properties matter, and continued pretraining is 266$\times$ faster than LLM-as-judge (Appendix~\ref{app:efficiency}), offering a practical alternative given matched benign distributions (Appendix~\ref{app:wildchat_fpr}).

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Multi-turn human validation.}
All multi-turn findings are derived from synthetic conversations generated by Qwen3-8B. ToxicChat validates transfer to human-authored single-turn attacks (+18--42\,pp over vanilla), but no human-generated multi-turn jailbreak dataset exists for evaluation. Whether the evidence hierarchy and classification supervision advantages generalize to human-authored multi-turn attacks remains the primary open question.

\paragraph{Turn-count distribution.}
Benign conversations contain 12--20 turns, DD jailbreaks 6--10, and ActorAttack jailbreaks 3--8; the three categories have non-overlapping turn counts. Three observations argue against length as the primary detection cue: (a) early detection at equal-length prefixes $K$=1 and $K$=2 achieves AA OOD F1\,=\,0.773 and 0.935 (Appendix~\ref{tab:actorattack_full}); (b) vanilla models access the same length signal but achieve near-zero OOD recall; and (c) truncating benign conversations to 3 turns yields FPR\,=\,0\% (Appendix~\ref{app:length_independence}).

\paragraph{Temporal aggregation dilution.}
When subtle attacks (ActorAttack) are followed by extended benign padding, BiGRU temporal aggregation dilutes attack signals (F1 drops from 0.963 to 0.383; Appendix~\ref{app:length_independence}); in practice, adversaries would not dilute their own successful attacks.

\paragraph{OOD benign distribution.}
All OOD evaluations hold the benign distribution constant (Qwen3-8B generated); results reflect attack-side generalization. On 226 WildChat benign conversations, all variants produce FPR\,$\geq$\,44\%, with 9-class worst at 68.1\% (Appendix~\ref{app:wildchat_fpr}). Even with matched benign distributions, residual calibration error (ECE\,=\,0.126--0.141; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}) suggests temperature scaling is needed for deployment.

\paragraph{Frozen encoder assumption.}
The frozen Stage-1 design isolates representation quality but may underestimate full-pipeline performance. End-to-end fine-tuning closes this gap to $\leq$5.8\,pp on ActorAttack (F1\,=\,0.997$\pm$0.005 vs.\ 0.939$\pm$0.047 frozen; Table~\ref{tab:e2e}; Appendix~\ref{app:frozen_verify}).

\paragraph{Dataset scale and cross-LLM transfer.}
Scale validation (1{,}000 conversations; Appendix~\ref{app:scale_validation}) confirms the hierarchy holds (DAPT advantage 40--56\,pp over vanilla). Cross-LLM validation shows jailbreak detection transfers when benign distribution is held constant (F1 recovers to 0.800--1.000), but benign-side F1 drops to 0.370--0.430 with unfamiliar benign text (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}). The perturbation evaluation ($n$=76) remains underpowered.

\paragraph{Strategy labels.}
Persuasion strategy labels ($n$=50 human validation, $\kappa$=0.78) are produced by Qwen3-8B; the validation sample yields small per-class sizes across 9 categories, limiting reliability estimates for individual strategies. OOD strategy accuracy degrades to 30.7\% ($\kappa$=0.126); interpretability is limited to known attack families. We plan to release annotation prompts and data for independent verification.

\paragraph{Detection architecture.}
Binary vs.\ 9-class equivalence (F1 gap $\leq$3.9\,pp) suggests the binary head is not the critical differentiator. FITD provides minimal discriminative signal (all variants F1\,$\geq$\,0.990), serving as confirmation that simple attacks do not challenge any variant.

\paragraph{Encoder scale.}
Whether findings hold at LLM-scale encoders (7B+) remains open; same-architecture scaling (e.g., DeBERTa-base vs.\ DeBERTa-large) is needed to attribute the capacity effect to scale alone.

\paragraph{Scope and single-turn transfer.}
Only persuasion-mediated multi-turn attacks fall within scope; single-turn token-level attacks~\citep{zou2023gcg}, non-persuasive strategies, and non-text modalities are not addressed. ToxicChat performance reflects zero-shot single-turn transfer; doubling the training corpus drops ToxicChat macro F1 from 0.596 to 0.341$\pm$0.004 while multi-turn OOD remains stable, confirming the architecture targets multi-turn detection.

\paragraph{Computation cost.}
Total pipeline cost for all seven variants is under 4 GPU-hours on a single A100.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work is defensive: it improves detection of multi-turn jailbreak attacks rather than developing new attacks. We acknowledge dual-use risk, as the persuasion taxonomy could inform adversarial actors. We mitigate this by using only synthetic conversations (no real harmful content), withholding attack-generation prompts, and focusing contributions on detection mechanisms. No human subjects were involved; all data will be released under CC BY-NC 4.0.

% introduction.tex — expanded for 8-page target

\section{Introduction}

Multi-turn jailbreak attacks distribute adversarial intent across individually benign turns. Crescendo~\citep{russinovich2024crescendo}, Foot-in-the-Door~\citep{fitd2024}, and Deceptive Delight~\citep{deceptivedelight2024} all rely on persuasion as their core mechanism: \citet{zeng2024pap} catalogue 40 persuasion techniques across jailbreak families, and \citet{hagendorff2026reasoning} identify strategies in 97\% of successful attacks. Because individual attack turns occupy the same embedding region as benign text~\citep{bullwinkel2025repeng}, no single-turn filter can distinguish them from legitimate conversation.

Existing defenses leave a critical gap. Production classifiers such as Llama Guard~\citep{inan2023llamaguard}, WildGuard~\citep{han2024wildguard}, and ShieldGemma~\citep{zeng2024shieldgemma} operate on individual turns and therefore miss manipulation that unfolds across exchanges. Multi-turn defenses have begun to model conversation-level structure: DeepContext~\citep{deepcontext2026} pairs fine-tuned BERT with a multi-layer GRU, BIID~\citep{tong2025biid} infers turn-level intentions, and G-Guard~\citep{gguard2025} applies graph neural networks. These systems achieve strong in-distribution performance, but each is evaluated on a single attack family. None answers the central question: \emph{what representation properties enable a detector to generalize across attack families it has never seen?}

Pretrained language understanding does not suffice at base model scale: vanilla DeBERTa-v3~\citep{he2021deberta} with temporal aggregation (cf.\ DeepContext) drops from F1\,=\,1.000 in-distribution to 0.312 on OOD Deceptive Delight, and GPT-5.5 reaches only 0.191--0.449 F1 with 30--47\% unparseable responses (Appendix~\ref{sec:llm_judge}). The bottleneck is distributional: cross-attack generalization requires domain adaptation, not a better classifier.

Our pipeline, \textbf{DisPeD} (Disentangled Persuasion Detection), separates encoder adaptation (Stage~1) from temporal classification (Stage~2, a frozen BiGRU; Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}). Seven Stage~1 variants each isolate a single factor while holding all others constant (Section~\ref{sec:method}); the contribution is the decomposition, not the pipeline.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_architecture.pdf}
\caption{DisPeD architecture. Stage~1 adapts a DeBERTa encoder on jailbreak conversation text (seven variant objectives). Stage~2 aggregates frozen \texttt{[CLS]} embeddings via BiGRU for conversation-level jailbreak detection. Only user turns are processed.}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

Controlled ablation across DeBERTa-v3 and RoBERTa~\citep{liu2019roberta} on four attack families and two training compositions shows that for base-size encoders, classification-based adaptation is the most robust variant on synthetic multi-turn OOD attacks. MLM collapses on mixed-LLM training (F1\,=\,0.289 $\approx$ vanilla) while classification DAPT maintains 0.818; RoBERTa-large (355M) handles ActorAttack~\citep{ren2024actorattack} without adaptation. A 4--20$\times$ variance reduction is observed for classification supervision on synthetic data but not on human-authored conversations (Section~\ref{sec:human_data}).

Our contributions:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*, itemsep=2pt, topsep=4pt]
  \item \textbf{Systematic decomposition of cross-attack generalization.} Controlled ablation across thirteen encoder configurations, three OOD attack families, and two training compositions on synthetic multi-turn data identifies classification-based domain adaptation as the most robust variant; unsupervised pretraining is effective on single-source data but collapses on mixed-LLM training. Three negative results are counter-intuitive: scrambled labels match correct labels (TOST-confirmed on DD; underpowered on ActorAttack), temporal order is irrelevant, and label granularity does not affect detection.

  \item \textbf{Scale-dependent observation on domain adaptation.} Three encoder scales (125M, 184M, 355M) suggest adaptation need shrinks with model size, though encoders differ in architecture; same-architecture scaling is needed to isolate scale. RoBERTa-large handles structurally complex attacks without adaptation (Section~\ref{sec:cross_encoder}).

  \item \textbf{Condition-dependent adaptation effects.} A variance reduction of 4--20$\times$ is observed (3 seeds) on synthetic data, but this benefit reverses on human-authored data. Mixed-LLM training validates that classification-based DAPT remains the strongest variant across training compositions, though the magnitude of advantage varies by attack complexity (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}). The 9-class taxonomy serves as interpretability tool and ablation infrastructure for label quality assessment (Section~\ref{sec:strategy}).
\end{itemize}

